format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

lesson radioactivity-is-natural
title: Radioactivity is natural
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u2-natural-sources
teaches: u2-not-always-dangerous
relies_on: u1-unstable-changes
relies_on: u2-decay-chain

description:
    Radioactivity was here long before people. You will find it in rocks, air, food, your own body and space, hear how it was discovered, and see why radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous.

cite ref-epa-radiation-sources
supports: Natural background radiation: radon, cosmic radiation and radioactive minerals in the ground and the body.

cite ref-openstax-up3-10-3
supports: Becquerel found in 1896 that a uranium-rich rock darkens a photographic plate.

cite ref-hps-natural-radioactivity
supports: Potassium-40 in the human body.

cite ref-epa-radioactive-decay
supports: Uranium and thorium decay chains in nature (optional card).

card c09
kind: teach
type: story
scored: 0
title: A discovery that wasn't supposed to happen
speaker: npc-ada
tag: 1896
figure: becquerel-plate
figure_at: 1

body:
    [[henri-becquerel|Henri Becquerel]] left a uranium mineral on a wrapped photographic plate and found it fogged, even with no sunlight. Radioactivity was found in a natural mineral, long before people made any nuclear technology.

card c08
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-tom-facilities
connected: true

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Rocks and soil
figure: rocks-soil
figure_at: 1

body:
    Rocks and soil contain some natural radioactive materials, such as uranium and thorium. This is why the ground gives off a little radiation. It has been this way since long before people.

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: In the air
figure: radon-house
figure_at: 1

body:
    Radioactive materials in the ground can produce [[radon|radon]], a radioactive gas. Radon is in the air outside, and it can get into buildings.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-choice-radon
connected: true

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Food and your body
figure: food-body
figure_at: 1

body:
    Your body contains a little natural radioactive potassium, called [[potassium-40|potassium-40]]. So do many foods, such as bananas. It is a tiny share of all your potassium.

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: From space
figure: cosmic-rays
figure_at: 1

body:
    Earth is hit by [[cosmic-radiation|cosmic radiation]] from space all the time. It is one more part of the [[background-radiation|radiation around us]].

card c07
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-choice-space
connected: true

card c10
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-order-first

card c12
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: It depends
figure: not-always-dangerous
figure_at: 1

body:
    Radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous. What matters is the type of radiation, how much there is, and how it reaches the body. A banana and your own body are radioactive, and that is normal. Later you will see why being exposed to radiation is not the same as becoming radioactive.

card c11
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-choice-dangerous
connected: true

card c13
kind: item
type: item
item: u2-l5-choice-decides

card c16
kind: teach
type: explain
tier: deeper
scored: 0
title: Go deeper: radon and chains
figure: radon-chain
figure_at: 1

body:
    Radon is one link in a natural decay chain that starts with uranium in rock. Potassium-40 is another natural radionuclide, found in food and the body. Natural chains like these were running long before people existed.
